  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CEDIG - CENTRE FOR DISABILITY RIGHTS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Spinal cord injury disrupts messages to and from the brain to the rest of the body! This results in paralysis, incontinence, pressure sores and sexual dysfunctions in men. This project aims to promote access to health services and improve the quality of life of spinal Injured persons.
Main objective: To facilitate Access to Health Services of 200 Persons with Spinal Injuries in Iganga and Jinja by2024
Changes to see:
Improved quality of life and improved Access to health services.

The project lead is Ms. Angela Balaba, she holds a Masters&#x2019; Degreein Business administration and management of Bugema University,majoring in project planning and management, a Bachelors&#x2019; Degreein Guidance and Counselling of Kyambogo University, majoring intrauma, sex and sexuality and a Post Graduate Diploma for non -financial managers of Uganda Management Institute. Angela hasundergone trainings in HIV/AIDs, palliative and pediatriccounselling, gender mainstreaming, human rights and disability,monitoring and evaluation, local government budgets processesand inclusiveness. Angela has vast experience in donor fundedprograms of fourteen (14) years, fundraising and report writing,project planning design and implementation, capacity building andmentorship. Angela has good knowledge and good insight ofinclusiveness of projectbeneficiaries. She is a swift, self -motivatedworker, who can work under tight deadlines unsupervised with goodcommunication skills, a good community mobiliser and a good teamplayer.
Ms. Monica Katumba is our accountant, She holders aBachelors&#x2019; Degree in Accounting and finance of MakerereUniversity.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CEDIG was began informally in 2016 by persons with Spinal Injuries, who became disabled through road traffic accidents. Besides thephysical disability there were other secondary complications like incontinence, sexual dysfunctions in men, hormonal imbalance and stress among others that needed a unified voice to advocate for our rights. CEDIG teamed with other road safety advocates to bring forth the voices of survivors as advocates for road safety.
We lobbied for a separate spinal ward/unit at Mulago National Referral hospital, as the mortality rate was high among spinal injured peoples due low immunity. Mixing them with other accident victims was easily transmitting multiple infections. The spinal unit led to peer counselling where our role models help newly injured people through the initial stages of mourning (shock, anger, denial, acceptance and coping). Later we go beyond the individual to include in family to fully understand the impact of the injury.
In 2018 we got registered legally as a company by guarantee as a pre-requisite by Ugandan laws. This helped us set up the secretariat and put in place all necessary requirements but it could not help us favorably compete for proposals, making us sub-grantees. Despite this we worked throughout the &#x2018;Covid 19&#x2019; pandemic distributing relief items, home visiting and counseling our members.
In 2021, CEDIG got fully registered as an NGO under INDR155054490NB and also get a Tax Identification Number.
The road traffic accidents are on the raise, killing and disabling lots of young people. The accidents do not discriminate women, men, young or old, hence CEDIG integrates gender into all her works because the roles of an individual person continue despite the disability.

CEDIG is formally registered within all relevant laws.
&#xf0d8; The spinal ward/unit is one of our biggest achievements! The mortality rates greatly reduced giving hope to the already injured persons to survive! It has both a male and female wing, giving ample privacy. Unfortunately its capacity is becoming small compared to the high rate of road traffic accidents.
&#xf0d8; Peercounselling and giving hope to a newly injured person is a big step ahead as it is more of a practical thing of (see me&#x2026;learn from me&#x2026;.I was like you!).
&#xf0d8; We have held Peer group trainings that bring together both newly injured persons and the seasoned role models. It helps in forming a sense of belonging, learning survival techniques as a wheelchair user with lots of secondary complications.
&#xf0d8; A few cases that needed compensation have been referred for legal help to Legal Action for Persons with disabilities (LAPD). The legal battle is still long and needs lots of interventions.
&#xf0d8; We are proud that we contributed towards the fight against &#x2018;Covid 19&#x2019;,despite the fact thatthe Ugandan Government did not consider us as vulnerable people.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Center for Disability Rights Resear</narrative>
